We do this if the midpoint value itself could potentially be the value we'r=
e looking
for and should still be <strong>included</strong> in the search space.</li>
</ul>
<p>The exclude/include logic applies when narrowing the search space toward=
s the left, as well (i.e., between <code>right =3D mid - 1</code> and <code=
>right =3D mid</code>).</p>
<p>Calculating the midpoint<br>
In most cases, the midpoint is calculated using <strong><code>mid =3D (left=
 + right) // 2</code></strong> in Python.<sup><a id=3D"user-content-fnref-1=
" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"footnote-label" href=3D"ht=
tps://bytebytego.com/courses/coding-patterns/binary-search/introduction-to-=
binary-search#user-content-fn-1">1</a></sup> To lower the risk of integer o=
verflow, <code>mid =3D left + (right - left) // 2</code> is preferred in ma=
ny other languages.</p>
<p><strong>3. Choosing an exit condition</strong><br>
Choosing an exit condition for when the while-loop should terminate can be =
tricky. Our choices are primarily between <code>left &lt; right</code> and =
<code>left =E2=89=A4 right</code>. Both conditions are applicable in differ=
ent situations.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>When the exit condition is <code>left &lt; right</code>, the while-loop =
will break when <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> meet.<sup><a id=3D=
"user-content-fnref-2" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"footn=
ote-label" href=3D"https://bytebytego.com/courses/coding-patterns/binary-se=
arch/introduction-to-binary-search#user-content-fn-2">2</a></sup></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>On the other hand, <code>left =E2=89=A4 right</code> ends once <code>lef=
t</code> has surpassed right.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents two diagrams illustrating different loop te=
rmination conditions in a search algorithm, likely binary search.  The left=
